{
  // Shared options come from the base config. What is genuinely
  // frontend-specific: the project reference to @marketpulse/shared, the
  // browser library surface, and the fact that this is the one package whose
  // JavaScript is produced by something other than tsc.
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "rootDir": "src",

    // No `outDir`, because there is no emit — see below. `dist/` in this
    // package belongs entirely to Vite.

    // TypeScript here typechecks and nothing else. Vite owns the artefact, so
    // a parallel tsc emit would be dead output that nobody imports, nobody
    // ships, and nothing typechecks against: unlike packages/shared, this is a
    // composite project *referenced by nothing*, so no consumer ever reads its
    // declarations.
    //
    // This is what resolves the collision the story flagged: `tsc -b` and Vite
    // both default to writing into `dist/`, and `tsc -b --clean` only knows
    // about the half it produced. With one producer there is no half to miss.
    // The cost is that `clean` can no longer be `tsc -b --clean` alone — see
    // the `clean` script in package.json.
    //
    // `composite` (from the base config) still applies and is still required:
    // the root solution references this project. `noEmit` alongside it is
    // accepted by `tsc -b` and still typechecks — verified against TypeScript
    // 6.0.3, and worth re-checking on an upgrade, because the combination was
    // an error in older versions.
    "noEmit": true,

    // The modern JSX transform (React 17+): a `.tsx` file needs no
    // `import React` to use JSX, because the compiler emits an import of
    // `react/jsx-runtime` itself. That matters more here than it looks —
    // `verbatimModuleSyntax` (base config) makes imports literal, so an
    // `import React` kept only to satisfy the classic transform would be a
    // real, unused runtime import rather than something erased.
    //
    // Sixth of the per-app compiler overrides and the second in this file
    // after `noEmit`. It belongs here and nowhere else: `packages/shared` is
    // consumed by the backend too, and JSX has no meaning there.
    //
    // Note this option is what tsc *typechecks* against, not what runs. The
    // transform that actually produces the JavaScript is the bundler's, since
    // tsc emits nothing in this package — so a disagreement between the two
    // shows up in the browser, not in a compile error.
    "jsx": "react-jsx",

    // --- The browser baseline ---
    //
    // Desktop-first, per PRODUCT_SPEC.md §3, and evergreen: current Chrome,
    // Edge, Firefox and Safari. ES2024 is the language level those four have
    // shared since roughly March 2024 (Safari 17.4 is the constraint;
    // Chrome 119 and Firefox 121 arrived earlier), which makes it about two
    // years of headroom at the time of writing rather than a bet on something
    // new. Task 1.1.4 left this pair provisional; this is the deliberate
    // version of it.
    //
    // It is also the same `target` the base config picks for Node 24, so the
    // workspace has one language level rather than two — but for a different
    // reason, and the two could legitimately diverge later.
    //
    // `target` has a second reader now: `build.target` in vite.config.ts
    // governs the downlevelling that actually ships. Change one and change the
    // other; Vite's default is lower than this and would apply silently.
    "target": "es2024",

    // The base config leaves `lib` unset so it follows `target`, so adding the
    // DOM means restating the language libraries alongside it.
    "lib": ["es2024", "dom", "dom.iterable"],

    // No Node globals here. Left empty rather than unset: without it, TypeScript
    // pulls in every @types package it can find, and pnpm's linking means
    // @types/node reaches this package's node_modules root anyway. `process`
    // and friends should not typecheck in browser code.
    "types": []
  },

  // index.html and vite.config.ts sit outside this deliberately. Vite reads
  // both directly and neither is part of the program tsc compiles.
  "include": ["src/**/*"],

  // See the note in apps/backend/tsconfig.json — this is what orders the build.
  "references": [{ "path": "../../packages/shared" }]
}
